<TopicPage slug="subjects/forensic-science/foundational/foundations-of-forensic-science">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Forensic science is applied science with a client: the courtroom. Its central premise is Edmond
    Locard's exchange principle — <strong>every contact leaves a trace</strong> — and its central
    discipline is making claims that survive hostile scrutiny. The science is only half the job;
    documentation, chain of custody and honest interpretation are the other half.
  </p>
  <p>
    The word "forensic" itself announces the double life: from the Latin <em>forum</em>, the
    marketplace where Romans argued their cases — forensic science is science performed
    <em>for the court</em>. That client changes everything. A research chemist publishes a result
    and invites peers to check it over months; a forensic scientist's result is checked by a
    defence expert within weeks, under procedures written into law, by someone paid to break it.
    Every rule this chapter teaches — document everything, preserve everything, claim no more
    than the method supports — exists because the adversary system is the field's peer review,
    conducted in public. It also gives the field a characteristic tension: the scientist's
    obligation is to the evidence, whatever it says; the client's interest is a verdict. Managing
    that tension honestly is the profession's defining skill.
  </p>
  <p>
    Start with the principle that makes the whole enterprise possible.
  </p>

  <h2>History and the Locard exchange principle</h2>
  <Widget no="1" caption="Locard's principle, worked through a burglary" widget="equationStep" config={locardCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>Locard's exchange principle</strong> — <em>tout contact laisse une trace</em>, every
    contact leaves a trace — is less a law of physics than a research programme: it asserts that
    transferred material is nearly always present, so the investigator's problem is detection,
    not existence. Edmond Locard (1877–1966), a physician and criminalist who founded the first
    police laboratory in Lyon (1910) in two attic rooms, built the discipline on it: he analysed
    dust from suspects' clothing, matched it to the places they had been, and proved the
    principle case by case. The principle's strength is also its discipline's warning — if every
    contact leaves a trace, then the <em>absence</em> of expected traces is itself evidence, and
    the scientist must be able to say which.
  </p>
  <p>
    The history around Locard is a history of better identification, and each era's revolution
    has been a better way to answer "who did this?". Before fingerprints, the Paris police
    measured bodies (<em>anthropometry</em>, Bertillon's system from 1879 — eleven measurements,
    plus the invention of the crime-scene photograph and the mugshot); Galton's statistical study
    of fingerprint individuality (1892) and the first fingerprint bureau (Argentina, 1891–92)
    replaced it; blood grouping (from 1901) narrowed suspects; and Alec Jeffreys's
    <strong>DNA fingerprinting</strong> (1984), with the first criminal conviction on DNA
    evidence in 1988 and national databases from 1995, raised identification to near-certainty —
    and, by comparison, quietly exposed how much of the older disciplines rested on judgement
    rather than statistics. The 2009 US National Academies (<em>NAS</em>) report made that
    exposure official: several pattern-matching disciplines (bite marks notably) lacked
    demonstrated error rates and scientific foundations, and the field has been rebuilding its
    statistical house ever since. Locard's two attic rooms grew into a profession, and the
    profession's growth spurts are exactly the timeline below.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="from Galton's prints to the NAS report" widget="timeline" config={historyCfg} />

  <h2>The forensic laboratory system</h2>
  <p>
    The work divides between two rooms, and the division is the system. The <strong>crime
    scene</strong> belongs to scene examiners (SOCOs in the UK; crime-scene investigators in the
    US), whose product is not conclusions but <em>preserved context</em>: secure the scene,
    document everything in situ (photography, sketching, notes — before anything is touched),
    search systematically (grids, spirals, zones — so coverage is provable), collect and package
    (each item separately, paper over plastic for biological material, because trapped moisture
    breeds the mould that destroys DNA), and record the <strong>chain of custody</strong> — every
    transfer of every item, signed and dated. The chain is what turns a physical object into
    evidence: an unbroken custody record survives cross-examination; a gap is a gift to the
    defence, and a broken chain has sunk otherwise airtight cases.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <strong>laboratory</strong> then sorts evidence by discipline. <em>Biology/DNA</em>
    screens for body fluids and develops profiles (short-tandem-repeat analysis; sensitivity
    now reaches a few cells). <em>Chemistry and toxicology</em> identifies controlled
    substances, analyses arson debris for accelerants, and measures drugs and poisons in blood
    and tissue (blood-alcohol and overdose work live here). <em>Trace evidence</em> handles the
    Locard material — fibres, glass, paint, gunshot residue, soil — mostly by
    microspectrophotometry, comparison microscopy and, increasingly, chemical profiling. <em>Firearms and toolmarks</em> compare striations and impressions; <em>fingerprints</em>
    (often a separate bureau) develop and compare latent prints, now with automated search
    (AFIS) proposing candidates but a human examiner making the comparison. <em>Document and
    digital</em> examination — handwriting, ink, and now terabytes of devices — round out the
    modern lab. Two features define the system's quality: laboratories are <em>accredited</em>
    to standards (ISO 17025; FEPAC for academic programmes), and their scientists are
    <em>independent of the investigation's outcome</em> — the answer belongs to the evidence,
    and a lab that reports "inconclusive" when a busy police force wanted "matching" is doing
    its job, not failing it.
  </p>

  <h2>Scientific method and evidence: class, individual and the claim you can make</h2>
  <p>
    Every comparison in forensic science splits evidence into two tiers. <strong>Class</strong>
    characteristics place evidence in a group: this fibre is blue acrylic, this shoe is a size 10
    training shoe, this paint chip matches a 2010s red Honda. <strong>Individual</strong>
    characteristics can tie evidence to a single source: random wear marks on a barrel, a full
    DNA profile, a fingerprint's unique ridge detail. The distinction is epistemological, not
    cosmetic — it is the boundary between "consistent with" and "came from" — and much of the
    discipline's history is the story of methods drifting between those tiers. DNA earned its
    place in the individual tier with population statistics: the chance two unrelated people
    share a full STR profile is astronomically small (billions to one), calculated from
    measured allele frequencies. The 2009 NAS report's warning was precisely that several
    pattern disciplines had drifted into source-level language without the equivalent
    statistics to support them.
  </p>
  <p>
    The scientific method enters at every step, but its purest form is the <strong>hypothesis
    test on physical evidence</strong>. Take a shooting reconstruction: the hypothesis "the
    shooter stood by the window" makes predictions — blood spatter angles, powder
    distribution, glass fall direction — and the scene either agrees or it does not; the
    scientist's job is to maximise what the evidence can discriminate between, and to record
    the <em>disconfirming</em> observation as carefully as the confirming one. Modern practice is converging on
    <strong>likelihood-ratio language</strong> — how much more likely is this evidence if the suspect is the source
    than if they are not? — which forces the expert to weigh both propositions and lets the
    court supply the priors. It pairs with admissibility law: US courts ask whether a method
    is testable, peer-reviewed, has a known error rate and general acceptance (the
    <em>Daubert</em> criteria, 1993, which replaced the older <em>Frye</em> "general
    acceptance" test); UK practice leans on expert-evidence rules to similar effect. That humility, plus rigorous documentation, is what separates science
    from theatre in the courtroom.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> forensic science solves crimes the way television shows
  it — one conclusive answer per episode, delivered in hours. Real casework is the opposite:
  analyses take weeks, results are probabilistic, evidence is often degraded, mixed or
  absent, and the most scientifically valuable statement is frequently "consistent with X,
  inconsistent with Y, cannot distinguish between Z and W". The "CSI effect" — juries
  expecting certainty — is a real concern in court management, and fighting it with honest
  likelihood language is part of the job.</p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Three frontier topics show where the foundations are being tested. First,
    <strong>trace evidence quantified</strong>: forensic statistics now borrows from
    Bayesian networks — combining multiple weak traces (a fibre here, glass there) into a
    single likelihood framework instead of reporting each in isolation, the standard
    response to the well-known danger of the "prosecutor's fallacy" (mistaking P(evidence |
    innocent) for P(innocent | evidence) — the two differ by the prior odds, and confusing
    them has produced documented miscarriages). Second, <strong>cognitive bias</strong>:
    laboratory studies show examiners can be influenced by case information they should not
    have (knowing the suspect confessed changes fingerprint conclusions); the remedy being
    adopted is <em>contextual management</em> — blind analysis, limiting what analysts are
    told, documenting when judgement was re-run blind. Third, <strong>the DNA frontier and
    its shadows</strong>: touch DNA from a few cells, probabilistic genotyping of three- and
    four-person mixtures, and investigative genetic genealogy (which caught the Golden State
    Killer in 2018, decades after the crimes) all expand what the exchange principle can
    harvest — while raising privacy questions the law is still settling. The through-line of
    all three: the field's foundational promise is not "we can find the answer" but "we can
    tell you exactly how much the evidence supports each answer" — a promise Locard's
    attic-lab successors are still learning to keep, discipline by discipline.
  </p>
  <p>
    A final worked case assembles the chapter's machinery in order, as it would run in
    practice. A warehouse burglary: padlock cut, glove fibres on a broken skylight frame,
    a muddy boot impression on the floor below. <em>Scene first:</em> examiners photograph
    and sketch before touching, lift the boot impression with electrostatic and casting
    methods, collect fibres with taped lifts, package everything separately in paper, and
    open the chain-of-custody log — none of this is analysis yet, but every later result
    depends on it. <em>Lab triage:</em> the boot impression yields <em>class</em>
    information (manufacturer, size, wear pattern — searchable in a footwear database,
    narrowing to a shortlist); the fibres are acrylic, matching the fibre composition of
    gloves sold by one supplier (class again); the padlock's cut faces carry striations
    compared under comparison microscopy against a suspect bolt cutter from a search —
    individual-level territory, argued with likelihood language and a documented error-rate
    discussion. <em>Interpretation:</em> the report states the findings as support —
    the impression is consistent with the shortlisted boot type, the fibres are
    indistinguishable from the gloves, the cut marks show correspondence consistent with
    the seized tool — and, just as importantly, states what was <em>not</em> found (no
    DNA-bearing contact traces recovered from the padlock, which limits — and honestly
    reports — how far the case can go). <em>Court:</em> the examiner presents method,
    validation and limits under cross-examination; the chain of custody is produced; the
    <em>Daubert</em>/admissibility questions are answerable because they were asked before
    the analysis, not after. Every stage of that vignette is one of this chapter's sections
    doing its job — which is the real definition of the foundations: not a list of famous
    firsts, but a sequence so routine it survives hostility, case after case.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> FEPAC accreditation standards (AAFS); National Research Council, <em>Strengthening Forensic Science in the United States: A Path Forward</em> (2009); Jackson &amp; Jackson, <em>Forensic Science</em>; Locard, <em>L'enquête criminelle et les méthodes scientifiques</em> (1920); President's Council of Advisors on Science and Technology forensics report (2016).</p>
